---
import Callout from "@luciodale/docs-ui-kit/components/Callout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "@luciodale/docs-ui-kit/components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ContentSection from "@luciodale/docs-ui-kit/components/ContentSection.astro";
import PropsTable from "@luciodale/docs-ui-kit/components/PropsTable.astro";
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro";
import { href } from "../../lib/link";

export const prerender = true;
---

<DocsPage title="Configuration">
	<ContentSection label="Configuration">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			The manager is configured through a single options object. Only three fields
			are required — a URL and a pair of serialization functions. Everything else
			has sensible defaults. Because the constructor is generic over your message
			types, every option is fully typed.
		</p>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Options at a Glance">
		<PropsTable
			rows={[
				{ name: "url", type: "string", description: "WebSocket endpoint URL", required: true },
				{ name: "serialize", type: "(msg: TClientMsg) => string", description: "Convert outgoing messages to strings", required: true },
				{ name: "deserialize", type: "(raw: string) => TServerMsg", description: "Parse incoming strings into typed messages", required: true },
				{ name: "ping", type: "() => TClientMsg", description: "Function returning the ping message, called each interval" },
				{ name: "isPong", type: "(msg: TServerMsg) => boolean", description: "Return true if an incoming message is a pong" },
				{ name: "pingIntervalMs", type: "number", defaultValue: "30000", description: "Milliseconds between pings" },
				{ name: "pongTimeoutMs", type: "number", defaultValue: "10000", description: "Milliseconds to wait for a pong before disconnecting" },
				{ name: "reconnectMaxAttempts", type: "number", defaultValue: "5", description: "Maximum reconnection attempts" },
				{ name: "reconnectBaseDelayMs", type: "number", defaultValue: "1000", description: "Initial reconnection delay" },
				{ name: "reconnectMaxDelayMs", type: "number", defaultValue: "5000", description: "Maximum reconnection delay" },
				{ name: "transport", type: "IWebSocketTransport", description: "Custom WebSocket transport for testing or non-browser environments" },
				{ name: "onMessageReceived", type: "(msg: TServerMsg) => void", description: "Called for every incoming message (except pongs)" },
				{ name: "onSendIntent", type: "(params: TSendParams) => void", description: "Called on every send(), even when offline" },
				{ name: "onConnectionStateChange", type: "(state: TConnectionState) => void", description: "Called when connection state changes" },
				{ name: "onReady", type: "() => void", description: "Called after (re)connect once subscriptions are restored" },
				{ name: "onInFlightDrop", type: "(messages) => void", description: "Called when in-flight messages are dropped on disconnect" },
				{ name: "onLastUnsubscribe", type: "(key, data) => void", description: "Called when the last subscriber for a key leaves" },
				{ name: "onDebug", type: "(event: TDebugEvent) => void", description: "Called for every internal event" },
			]}
		/>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Required Options">
		<CodeBlock
			code={`const manager = new WebSocketManager<TClientMsg, TServerMsg>({
  // The WebSocket endpoint
  url: "wss://api.example.com/ws",

  // How to turn your typed messages into strings for the wire
  serialize: (msg) => JSON.stringify(msg),

  // How to turn incoming strings back into typed messages
  deserialize: (raw) => JSON.parse(raw),
});`}
			language="tsx"
			filename="manager.ts"
		/>
		<p class="text-white/60 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
			The <code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">serialize</code> and
			<code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">deserialize</code> functions are typed against
			your generics, so TypeScript ensures you're producing and consuming the right shapes.
			You can use JSON, MessagePack, Protobuf — whatever your server speaks.
		</p>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Ping / Pong">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			Keep-alive pings prevent idle connections from being dropped by proxies
			or load balancers. The <code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">ping</code> option is
			a function, so you can include dynamic values like timestamps:
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			code={`{
  // Called on every interval tick — return a fresh ping message
  ping: () => ({ action: "ping", timestamp: Date.now() }),

  // Return true if an incoming message is a pong
  isPong: (msg) => msg.action === "pong",

  // How often to send pings (default: 30000ms)
  pingIntervalMs: 30_000,

  // How long to wait for a pong before disconnecting (default: 10000ms)
  pongTimeoutMs: 10_000,
}`}
			language="tsx"
			filename="manager.ts"
		/>
		<Callout type="info">
			If <code>isPong</code> is provided and the server doesn't respond within
			<code>pongTimeoutMs</code>, the connection is closed and reconnection begins
			automatically.
		</Callout>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Reconnection">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			When a connection drops unexpectedly, the manager automatically reconnects
			using exponential backoff with jitter. See the
			<a href={href("/docs/reconnection")} class="text-accent hover:underline">Reconnection</a> guide for the full picture.
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			code={`{
  // Maximum reconnect attempts before giving up (default: 5)
  reconnectMaxAttempts: 5,

  // Initial delay before the first retry (default: 1000ms)
  reconnectBaseDelayMs: 1_000,

  // Cap on the delay between retries (default: 5000ms)
  reconnectMaxDelayMs: 5_000,
}`}
			language="tsx"
			filename="manager.ts"
		/>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Callbacks as Dispatchers">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			Callbacks are where your application logic lives. Think of them as dispatchers —
			each one handles a specific moment in the message lifecycle. The manager calls
			them at the right time; you decide what to do:
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			code={`{
  // The main dispatcher. Branch on message type to handle each variant.
  // This is where you update your store, ack in-flight messages, and
  // resolve pending subscriptions.
  onMessageReceived(msg) {
    switch (msg.action) {
      case "subscribe_ack":
        manager.resolvePendingSubscription(\`chat:\${msg.channel}\`);
        break;
      case "message":
        manager.ackInFlight(msg.id);
        store.addMessage(msg);
        break;
    }
  },

  // Fires on every send() — even when offline.
  // The right place for optimistic UI updates.
  onSendIntent({ data, ackId }) {
    if (data.action === "message" && ackId) {
      store.addOptimistic(data, ackId);
    }
  },

  // Connection state changes: "disconnected" | "connecting" | "connected" | "reconnecting"
  onConnectionStateChange(state) {
    console.log("Connection:", state);
  },

  // Fires after a (re)connect once subscriptions have been restored.
  // Good place to sync any state you may have missed while offline.
  onReady() {
    fetchMissedMessages();
  },

  // In-flight messages that never got confirmed before a disconnect.
  // Save them for retry or mark them as failed.
  onInFlightDrop(messages) {
    for (const { id } of messages) {
      store.markFailed(id);
    }
  },

  // The last subscriber for a key left. Clean up related state.
  onLastUnsubscribe(key, data) {
    store.clearChannel(key);
  },

  // Every internal event — useful for debugging and the inspector panel.
  onDebug(event) {
    console.log(event.type, event);
  },
}`}
			language="tsx"
			filename="manager.ts"
		/>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Custom Transport">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			By default the manager uses the browser's native <code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">WebSocket</code>.
			You can provide a custom transport that implements
			<code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">IWebSocketTransport</code> — useful for
			testing or non-browser environments:
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			code={`import type { IWebSocketTransport } from "@luciodale/react-socket";

const customTransport: IWebSocketTransport = {
  connect(url, protocols) { /* ... */ },
  disconnect(code, reason) { /* ... */ },
  send(data) { /* ... */ },
  readyState: 0,
  onopen: null,
  onclose: null,
  onmessage: null,
  onerror: null,
};

const manager = new WebSocketManager<TClientMsg, TServerMsg>({
  url: "wss://api.example.com/ws",
  serialize: (msg) => JSON.stringify(msg),
  deserialize: (raw) => JSON.parse(raw),
  transport: customTransport,
});`}
			language="tsx"
			filename="manager.ts"
		/>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Next Steps">
		<ul class="space-y-2 text-white/60">
			<li>
				<a href={href("/docs/api")} class="text-accent hover:underline">API Reference</a> — every method and type
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href={href("/docs/subscriptions")} class="text-accent hover:underline">Subscriptions</a> — channels with automatic deduplication
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href={href("/docs/optimistic-updates")} class="text-accent hover:underline">Sending Messages</a> — fire-and-forget and acknowledged sends
			</li>
		</ul>
	</ContentSection>
</DocsPage>
